<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gulf of Mexico</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> is an arm of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and other great rivers drain into it, and explorers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-robert-cavelier-sieur-de-la-salle">La Salle</a>, who reached the Mississippi's mouth in 1682, used it to claim the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Its ports, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>, linked the Spanish and French empires in America with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Gulf of Mexico" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulf_of_Mexico_ETOPO_2022_(labeled).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
